    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-c51074b67df0\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"error-code-field-not-screaming-snake\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"^\\\\s*(public\\\\s+|protected\\\\s+|private\\\\s+)?(static\\\\s+)?(override\\\\s+)?(readonly\\\\s+)?code\\\\s*(:[^=]*)?=\\\\s*['\\\"][^'\\\"]*[^A-Z0-9_'\\\"][^'\\\"]*['\\\"]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.ts*\"},{\"id\":\"error-ctor-code-not-screaming-snake\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"new [A-Za-z0-9_]*Error\\\\(.*\\\\bcode:\\\\s*['\\\"][^'\\\"]*[^A-Z0-9_'\\\"][^'\\\"]*['\\\"]\",\"path\":\".floor/added/**/*.ts*\"}],\"coverage\":\"Lines added to .ts/.tsx files that give an error a literal `code` that is not SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE. This covers two forms: a class field written as `readonly code = 'invalid-email'`, and a `code:` value passed on the same line to a `new XxxError(...)` constructor.\",\"residual\":\"Does the change handle an expected failure without a typed domain error carrying a stable SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE `code` that callers can switch on exhaustively? Examples: a generic Error, a string or null result, a non-literal or non-unique code, or a code type that is not a closed union.\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/domain/email-error.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,3 @@\\n+export class InvalidEmailError extends Error {\\n+  readonly code = 'invalid-email';\\n+}\\n\"},{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/domain/stock.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,4 @@\\n+import { OrderError } from './errors';\\n+export function reserve(qty: number): void {\\n+  if (qty <= 0) throw new OrderError('Out of stock', { code: 'outOfStock' });\\n+}\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"patch\":\"--- /dev/null\\n+++ b/src/domain/email-error.ts\\n@@ -0,0 +1,6 @@\\n+export class InvalidEmailError extends Error {\\n+  readonly code = 'INVALID_EMAIL' as const;\\n+}\\n+export function reserve(qty: number): void {\\n+  if (qty <= 0) throw new InvalidEmailError('bad', { code: 'OUT_OF_STOCK' });\\n+}\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Single-line patterns can mechanically flag a literal error code that is not in SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE. They cannot decide whether a failure is expected rather than a bug, whether a generic Error or a string return counts as untyped handling, whether a code is stable, or whether callers can switch on the codes exhaustively. Those questions need semantic and multi-line judgement, so the rule is partial.\"}",
